-- erwartet: C001
-- **642 -- a `forever` loop is the whole body of a function that returns a value.**
--
-- The loop never leaves, so Gabbro needs no `return` after it, and the checker is right to
-- say so: `5 items, 0 errors, 0 hints`. The emitter lowered it to `for (;;) { … }` and
-- stopped there, which is the shape a C programmer would write by hand -- and
-- `cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror` answers
--
--     no return statement in function returning non-void [-Werror=return-type]
--
-- **GCC's `-Wreturn-type` is syntactic at the front end**, measured on the plain C beside
-- the generated file: `static uint64_t g(void) { for (;;) { x = 1; } }` draws the same
-- error with no Gabbro anywhere near it. So this is not a quirk of what the emitter wrote
-- around the loop; it is the gate this folder compiles every unit under.
--
-- **The corpus never showed it, and the reason is measurable**: `pruefe-emission.sh`
-- reports 117 of 117 units compiling, and not one of them puts a `forever` in a function
-- that answers. *A gate over a corpus measures the corpus.*
--
-- > Found 2026-09-02 by `instrumente/fuzze-erzeuger.py`: 65 of 65 accepted rungs of the
-- > `forever-schranke` form failed the compile gate, at every bound from `1` to `2^127`.
-- > **The bound is not the defect** -- the swept slot has nothing to do with it, and that is
-- > exactly the class a form-wise sweep is good at showing: one answer at every rung means
-- > the finding sits in the FORM.
--
-- THE REPAIR, 2026-09-03 -- a refusal that names a word the language already has
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `__builtin_unreachable();` after the loop was the candidate the finding named, and it
-- lowers a declaration that is not true. **`-> u64` says this function hands back a `u64`,
-- and nothing here ever does.** `never` is in the vocabulary, `M2` reads it, and
-- `prototyp_kern` has lowered it to `_Noreturn void` since the day `exit()` got one. The
-- same file with `-> never` and `diverges` in its effects was written out and run before
-- the rule was built: checker silent, emitter exit `0`, `cc` silent. *So this is the one
-- case where refusing beats lowering -- the refusal points at a form the author can write.*
--
-- **And the rule is GCC's own, on purpose: a body that holds no `return` ANYWHERE.** Not a
-- reachability analysis and not pretending to be one. That makes it exact in both
-- directions at once:
--
--     for (;;) { x = 1; }        no `return` in the body   -- both refuse
--     for (;;) { return 1; }     a `return` in the body    -- both accept
--
-- A `forever` with a `return` inside it answers, and nothing here touches it.
--
-- > **The defect turned out to be wider than the loop that carries it**, measured the same
-- > day: `impl fn g() -> u64 { erledigt = 1; }` -- no loop anywhere -- reached `cc` with the
-- > same error under `0 errors` and exit `0`. One rule covers both, because it is one rule.
-- > Over the 612 `.gab` files of the tree the new refusal changes the answer for exactly
-- > this one.

module gift::forever_in_a_function_that_answers {

static mut erledigt : u64 = 0;

assume zeitgeber_tickt "the timer ticks" falsifier wachhund_schlug_an;

extern fn wachhund_schlug_an() -> never
    effects { diverges };

impl fn g() -> u64
    effects { reads erledigt, writes erledigt }
{
    forever runde
        per_pass bounded 4096 ops
        on_exceeded wachhund_schlug_an
        effects { reads erledigt, writes erledigt }
        progress zeitgeber_tickt
    {
        erledigt = 1;
    }
}

}
